IBM Bobathon: Build for Bengaluru
Build with Bob. Better Bengaluru.

Bring developers and students together to build solutions for a city that moves better, creates more opportunities and cares for its communities—with IBM Bob at the centre of the building journey.

The challenge is organised into three themes, with a student category across all three.

1. Smarter Streets Better Living

How can agentic AI help solve Bengaluru’s everyday civic challenges?

Potential solutions: Traffic congestion and commute planning; traffic violation prevention through road-safety nudges; waste segregation and collection; pothole reporting and resolution tracking; water conservation; neighbourhood flood alerts.

2. More Opportunity Greater Together

How can technology expand access to employment and help community initiatives reach more people?

Potential solutions: Multilingual job and apprenticeship matching; skill-gap identification and learning pathways; digital support for local businesses; volunteer and NGO coordination; surplus food redistribution; neighbourhood skill and resource exchanges.

3. Safer Communities Stronger Futures

How can technology make everyday life safer and more inclusive for children, women, seniors and people with disabilities?

Potential solutions: Safer school journeys and authorised pickup coordination; cyberbullying reporting and support; women’s travel safety tools; senior check-ins and scam awareness; accessible public-space navigation; trusted community assistance networks.

4. Student Category

Start with a problem you see. Build a solution your community can use.

Students can enter any of the above themes. There is only one dedicated prize for Students.

Participation Requirements

Make “Built with IBM Bob” the core participation requirement. Each team should submit:

The problem: Who experiences it, and what needs to improve?
The prototype: A working solution built using IBM Bob, with agentic AI where useful.
The Bob story: A short demo showing how the team used Bob throughout development.
The impact: One measurable outcome, such as time saved, waste reduced or people connected to opportunities.
For safety-focused projects, include privacy and human review in the design.


Judging Criteria

Eligible Submissions will be evaluated using the following criteria:

(a) Completeness and Feasibility (5 points).

Judges will evaluate the feasibility of the solution, completeness of the concept and implementation, quality of the proof-of-concept, and effectiveness of the Participant's use of required IBM Technology.

(b) Creativity and Innovation (5 points).

Judges will evaluate the originality of the solution, uniqueness of the approach, degree of innovation, and effectiveness of the Participant's use of AI and IBM Technology in addressing the challenge.

(c) Design and Usability (5 points).

Judges will evaluate the quality of the user experience, accessibility, usability, ease of adoption, and effectiveness of the proposed design in real-world scenarios.

(d) Effectiveness and Efficiency (5 points).

Judges will evaluate how effectively the Submission addresses the challenge, potential real-world impact, scalability, efficiency, and ability to solve meaningful user problems.


Each criterion will be scored on a five (5) point scale, for a maximum possible score of twenty (20) points. A Submission's final score will be the average of the judges' scores. Subject to verification and compliance with these Rules, the highest-scoring eligible Submission(s) will be selected as winners. IBM and TechGig reserve the right to publish supplemental judging guidance, scoring examples, or explanatory materials that are consistent with these criteria. Judges may consider the clarity of the problem statement, practicality of the solution, prototype readiness, and expected impact when evaluating Submissions under the existing criteria.